  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 302 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSynoptically, starting Thursday morning, an upper-level ridge is \nforecast to be positioned along the Southern US / Gulf border. \nEnsemble guidance and deterministic models place the ridge axis \nalong the Missouri River Valley region, establishing southwesterly \nupper level flow across the Tri-State Area. In addition to the ridge \nto the south, both the GFS and Canadian-NH bring a shortwave through \nearly Thursday morning, accompanied by an associated vorticity \nmaximum, and jet streak. Additional shortwaves may move through the \nregion during the latter part of the period as the ridge begins to \nshift westward.\n\nHigh temperatures for the week are forecast to mainly in the 70s. \nOVer the weekend, expect temperatures to briefly warm into the low \nto mid 80s across the majority of the CWA. Sustained winds and gusts \nare forecast to stay under 25 mph, prevailing mainly out of the south \nto southeast. Friday looks to be the windiest, with gusts up to 20 \nmph. \n\nPrecipitation chances will be higher late in the week before \ntapering off over the weekend. As the shortwave move through early \nThursday morning, Probabilities of Precipitation (PoPs) are \ncurrently forecast at 60-80% for most of the Tri-State area as a \nsurface low with an associated boundary/front passes through. For \nFriday, guidance hints at a surface low developing out of Colorado; \nhowever, model agreement varies, with the ECMWF and GFS showing the \nlow while the Canadian and ensemble members, like the LREF, do not. \nWhere this low develops and tracks will impact Friday and \npotentially Saturday morning PoPs. Friday's PoPs are forecast in the \n20-50% range, potentially ending early Saturday morning. Saturday \nafternoon through Monday PoPs remain around 15-30% each\nafternoon.",
